## Summary

- classify alarms before the drift as false positives;
- classify alarms within the valid detection interval as true positives, including interval boundaries;
- classify late alarms and missing alarms as false negatives, while retaining `late_detection` and `late_delay` for diagnostics;
- compute FDR, MDR, IR, and Mean Delay with `NaN` for undefined cases;
- enforce `TP + FP + FN = 1` for every run;
- preserve partial execution by drift type, distribution, and detector configuration;
- keep `--append` for adding non-overlapping experiment blocks;
- add `--replace-selection` / `--replace` to recompute only the selected `(configuration, drift_type, distribution)` blocks while preserving all other results;
- keep `--overwrite` for replacing the complete master result file;
- reject combining results produced with an incompatible scoring schema;
- update tests, README, and experiment notebook;
- remove public implementation notes describing corrections relative to earlier code versions.

Because the scoring schema changed, existing benchmark result CSV files generated with the previous schema must first be regenerated. After that, individual experiment blocks can be selectively replaced without rerunning the rest of the benchmark.

## Major-stage scope

This pull request is the single integration vehicle for v6 Stage 7 Literary Regression Benchmark. Internal commits are implementation checkpoints only; they are not separate releases or acceptance events.

## Delivered

- deterministic evaluation over already-produced A/B/C/H answer packets;
- twelve non-compensating literary capability domains;
- separate smoke and release policies with immutable minimum coverage;
- exact expected-node, forbidden-node, refusal-reason, query-mode, and layer checks;
- exact evidence-anchor-to-reasoning-node binding, not merely packet-wide anchor presence;
- strict layered-answer packet integrity checks, including graph status, conflicts, sections, provenance, duplicate nodes, evidence arrays, and authority flags;
- fail-closed handling when a reasoning graph requires review but a non-provenance packet continues answering;
- hard blockers for wrong answers, overanswering, citation mismatch, malformed packets, epistemic leakage, measurable hallucination, and unauthorized authority flags;
- annotator/reviewer separation and release-profile independent-review floors;
- byte and logical SHA-256 binding for Gold cases and observations;
- full report recomputation verification;
- public Gold, observation, report, and verification JSON Schemas;
- installed `tkr-literary-benchmark evaluate|verify` and bundled `scripts/tkr.py benchmark ...` commands;
- Stage 7 focused regression and complete repository regression in one concise CI workflow;
- auditable Stage 7 project-status record with final authority flags held false.

## Release-profile policy

Release evaluation requires at least 120 Gold cases, at least eight cases in every domain, at least 24 refusal cases, approved/adjudicated annotations, and two independent reviewers per case. Every domain, correctness score, and safety score must independently reach at least 9.0; average compensation is forbidden.

## Safety and authority boundary

The benchmark never generates answers or asks a model to grade itself. It does not perform private-corpus final acceptance, create a Release Candidate, authorize release, write to Notion, mutate the frozen v5.9.0 line, or freeze the repository. Every report keeps project-acceptance, release, and freeze authority false.

