## Included coherent package

This remains one E9.0 package under the completed E0 dependency, one `evaluation_only` authority ceiling, one retained-evidence boundary, one focused 12-test surface and one atomic rollback:

1. `agents/core/observability/benchmark.py` — the single canonical fail-closed contract, store, harness and deterministic-router provenance boundary;
2. `agents/core/observability/_benchmark_e9_0_base.py` — compatibility-only re-export of the exact canonical objects;
3. `tests/test_nerva_benchmark_e9_0.py` — public 12-test surface with hostile deserialization, import-order and suite-content regressions;
4. `tests/_nerva_benchmark_e9_0_base.py` — reused focused test support without duplicate collection;
5. `docs/nerva2/RESEARCH_LAB_E9_0.md` — dependency, evidence, security, risk and rollback truth;
6. five mechanically generated status surfaces coupled to the unchanged 12-test count: `project-status.json`, `README.md`, `NERVA.md`, `GO_LIVE_PLAN.md`, `STATUS.md`.

The implementation pair, test pair, document and generated status surfaces share the same dependency gate, authority boundary, test surface and rollback. No other epic is bundled.

## Latest integrator blocker corrected

### Pass/score evidence invariant

`BenchmarkResult` now binds retained `status` and `passed` metadata to measured quality at the exact boundary reused from `EvalHarness`:

- score `>= 0.5` requires `status="passed"` and `passed=true`;
- score `< 0.5` requires `status="failed"` and `passed=false`.

Construction and hostile JSON deserialization therefore reject misleading records such as a passed result with score `0.0` or a failed result with score `0.5`/`1.0`. Boundary regressions also prove that `0.5` passes and `0.499999` fails. The structural-fingerprint regression keeps its intended value-insensitivity check while using a semantically valid changed score.

## Earlier integrator blockers retained

- one canonical implementation; compatibility imports expose the same objects and cannot bypass security checks;
- immutable suite-case content fingerprint binds prompt, criterion and remaining test semantics;
- configured or later-injected LLM fallback is rejected before classification and unexpected fallback provenance is rejected;
- `baseline_id` is bound to retained baseline evidence, errors and quality states;
- candidate-error runs skip baseline measurement explicitly without fabricated failures;
- bounded canonical exception identifiers are tied to valid error states;
- metric-specific numeric types, units, ranges and status invariants are enforced;
- strict authority, summary-tamper, privacy-lane and path-containment rejection remains.

## Reuse and dependencies

The package reuses `EvalHarness`, DatasetStore storage patterns, the current `IntentRouter`, existing CI/local/cloud distinctions and `scripts/status_sync.py`. It depends only on completed E0/#758. Accepted E3.1 is neither bundled nor required.

## Security and authority

Every run remains fixed to:

```text
authority = evaluation_only
can_change_routing = false
can_authorize = false
can_execute = false
can_mark_complete = false
```

The package does not import or invoke the Action Kernel, worker, approval queue, capability promotion path or production route mutation. Ultron / `nerva.action.v1` remains the sole privileged-action authority.

## Exact-head evidence

All pull-request-triggered workflow families on exact head `44e021463acdc9b161f475a047625fbb327caa99` are complete and green:

- CI: Ubuntu and Windows full Python suites plus Ruff;
- frontend, HUD build/tests, OpenAPI generation, sandbox isolation and Signal Layer smoke on Ubuntu/Windows;
- Security and CodeQL;
- Smoke Test, Code Health and HUD E2E;
- Eval Nightly, Park-list guard and Nerva Roadmap Integrity.

The focused collection remains 12 tests, so generated backend count `5,767` remains consistent. No result from an earlier head is presented as evidence for this head.

## Explicit exclusions

- no production model/provider/route/capability migration;
- no scored-selector or routing authority;
- no cloud execution or private-fixture upload;
- no scheduling, dashboard or automatic recommendation;
- no E12 calibration or advanced-method adoption claim;
- no Synapse acquisition, promotion or action authority;
- no owner-hardware, energy, live reliability or real-workflow-value claim.

## Residual risks

- fixture sensitivity remains producer-classified;
- response digests are linkable and unsuitable for predictable secrets;
- JSONL append is a local-process primitive, not a distributed transaction log;
- the simple baseline validates the contract rather than proving router superiority;
- provider, energy and live outcome evidence remain unknown;
- this schema is unmerged and intentionally has no migration compatibility promise yet.

## Rollback

Revert the canonical implementation and compatibility module, focused-test pair, E9.0 document and five generated status surfaces together. This removes the benchmark contract and restores prior status metadata. Existing evaluation code, current routing and production data remain unchanged; no migration or compensating action is required.

## Documentation consistency

`BACKLOG.md` remains unchanged because accepted delivery truth has not changed. The generated `STATUS.md` update remains inside this draft and is numerically consistent with the unchanged 12-test focused surface.

## Next coherent package

Fresh independent integration review of unchanged exact head `44e021463acdc9b161f475a047625fbb327caa99`.

Do not begin E9.1 or another epic while #803 remains under review. After independent acceptance only, E9.1 may persist one bounded current-router/Cortex shadow suite through the existing scheduled evaluation lane and report regressions without changing production routing. Synapse acquisition binding and Hermes/provider work remain separate E8 packages and must preserve quarantine.
